## Key Analytical Observations

**Growth momentum (FY24 → FY25):**
Star Health remained the largest standalone health insurer across all periods, but its growth of **+9.6%** trailed peers — Care Health grew **+21.2%**, Niva Bupa **+20.6%**, and Aditya Birla Health led with **+30.3%**. This pattern suggests share erosion at the market leader even as the standalone health segment as a whole expanded.

**Industry-wide growth:**
All-India industry Health GDPI rose from **₹120,961cr** (FY24) to **₹130,680cr** (FY25), a **+8.0%** YoY increase. Since standalone health insurers collectively grew faster than this industry average, they gained combined market share relative to multi-line general insurers and specialised players over the period.

**FY26 caveat:**
The FY26 figures reflect only **Q1 (April–June 2026)** cumulative premium and are **not directly comparable** to the full-year FY24/FY25 figures. If used as a directional proxy for full-year run-rate, a rough ~4x annualization could be applied, but this should be treated with caution given the seasonal and quarterly variability inherent in health insurance premium collection (e.g., renewal cycles, group scheme timing).

**Data integrity note:**
All figures were extracted strictly from the GI Council's Health Portfolio sheet, using each insurer's "Grand Total" column (covering Health-Retail, Health-Group, Government schemes, and Overseas Medical sub-segments) — no company press releases were used. All five standalone insurers had published figures for every requested period, so no values required omission.</ans>
